.Dd August 14, 2026
.Dt LOGIN_STNS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm login_stns
.Nd authenticate a password against an STNS directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl s Ar service
.Op Fl v Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
.Ar user
.Op Ar class
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a
.Xr login.conf 5
authentication style that checks a password against the hash an
.Lk https://stns.jp/ STNS
API server holds for the user.
.Pp
.Xr ypstns 8
answers who somebody is and what they are allowed to be.
This answers whether a password is theirs, which on
.Ox
is a separate question asked through a separate mechanism:
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr sshd 8
and everything else that authenticates a person calls
.Xr authenticate 3 ,
which runs this program and believes what it says.
Nothing links against it and nothing has to be told about it twice - every
caller goes the same way.
.Pp
It is not run by hand.
The options are the ones
.Xr authenticate 3
passes:
.Fl s
selects
.Cm login ,
.Cm challenge
or
.Cm response ,
and
.Fl v
carries values from
.Xr login.conf 5 ,
none of which mean anything here.
.Sh SETTING IT UP
Install the style where
.Xr login 1
looks for it.
The path is fixed in libc, and it is outside
.Ev LOCALBASE ,
so the port cannot write it and leaves the program under
.Ev LOCALBASE
for an administrator to link:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ln -s /usr/local/libexec/auth/login_stns /usr/libexec/auth/login_stns
.Ed
.Pp
Then add a class to
.Xr login.conf 5
and put the users in it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
stns:\e
	:auth=passwd,stns:\e
	:tc=default:
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm passwd
comes first, and the order is the whole of the safety here.
A local account is answered out of
.Xr master.passwd 5
without the API being asked at all, so root can still log in when the API
server is unreachable - the same reasoning that puts the local files ahead of
the directory everywhere else in this family.
.Pp
Users get the class from the directory or from
.Xr master.passwd 5 ;
.Xr ypstns 8
leaves the class field empty, so a directory user takes
.Cm default
unless
.Xr login.conf 5
says otherwise.
To apply the style to everyone, put
.Cm auth=passwd,stns
in
.Cm default
instead of making a class.
.Pp
Check it with
.Xr su 1 ,
which is the shortest path that exercises the whole thing:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ su -l alice
.Ed
.Sh HASHES
The password is compared against the hash the directory holds, by the same
code
.Xr ypstns 8
links.
That code carries its own SHA-256 crypt and SHA-512 crypt, because
.Xr crypt 3
on this system understands bcrypt and nothing else, and the hashes an STNS
directory carries are almost always SHA-512 crypt - they are generally created
on a Linux host.
.Pp
So
.Ql $6$ ,
.Ql $5$
and
.Ql $2b$
all work here.
Anything else is refused, and the refusal is logged as
.Dq no usable password hash
rather than as a wrong password, because an administrator whose users cannot
log in needs to know which of the two it is.
.Sh SECURITY
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Failure is always a rejection
Every outcome that is not a positive match is a rejection: an unreachable API,
a hash of a kind that cannot be read, a locked account, a name outside the
character set STNS permits, a configuration that will not load.
The caller is told the same thing in each case and the reason goes to
.Xr syslogd 8 .
.It An empty password is refused before it is hashed
The hash of an empty string is a perfectly good hash, and an account that
happened to hold one would otherwise be open to anybody who pressed return.
.It The process is confined
.Xr unveil 2
reduces the filesystem to the resolver configuration, the trust store and
whatever TLS material
.Xr stns.conf 5
names -
.Xr stns.conf 5
itself having already been read - and
.Xr pledge 2
reduces the rest to
.Qq stdio rpath inet dns tty .
Core dumps are turned off, because this process holds a password in memory for
as long as it takes to hash it.
.It Setuid root, as every style on this system is
.Xr authenticate 3
may be called by a program that is not root, and this has to read a
.Xr stns.conf 5
that may hold an API token and be readable by nobody else - so it is installed
setuid root and group
.Sy auth ,
which is exactly how
.Pa /usr/libexec/auth/login_passwd
is installed and for the same reason.
The confinement above is what keeps that honest: by the time anything of the
caller's is looked at, the filesystem is four files and the process cannot
execute, write, or open anything new.
.It The password goes to the machine, not to the API
Only the hash is fetched; the comparison happens here.
The API is never sent a password and never asked whether one is correct.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/libexec/auth/login_stns -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/libexec/auth/login_stns
Where the port installs it.
.It Pa /usr/libexec/auth/login_stns
Where
.Xr login 1
looks for it; a symbolic link to the above.
.It Pa /etc/stns/client/stns.conf
The STNS API client configuration, shared with
.Xr ypstns 8
and
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 .
.It Pa /etc/login.conf
Where the style is named.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr login 1 ,
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr authenticate 3 ,
.Xr login.conf 5 ,
.Xr stns.conf 5 ,
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 ,
.Xr ypstns 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An zakinko Aq Mt zakinko@snowrabbit.org
.Sh CAVEATS
Authentication needs the API server, every time.
There is no cached credential and there is deliberately no fallback to one: a
password checked against something this machine remembered is a password
checked against something that may since have been revoked.
An account that has to work while the directory is unreachable wants a local
entry in
.Xr master.passwd 5 ,
which
.Cm auth=passwd,stns
already prefers.
